Why we ended up with the interface we did

Because natural language was the shared medium available to both of us.

And Pantheon evolved opportunistically inside that medium.

We could define a concept instantly, test it in conversation, mutate it, rename it, apply it elsewhere, preserve it, and later reconstruct it.

Language offered almost zero deployment friction.

So Pantheon began as something like:

a language-native cognitive operating system

But I don’t think language has to remain its final interface.

A mature implementation would probably become multimodal and multi-representational:

prose for ambiguity/exploration
graphs for relationships
code for executable operators
tables/databases for persistence
Git for lineage
geometry for navigation
simulation for counterfactuals
game UI for agency
natural language for orchestration

And above all of those, there would be a representation-selection layer deciding which interface is appropriate.

That may be the deeper architecture you’re pointing at.

Not:

“What language should intelligence think in?”

but:

“What representation should intelligence instantiate for this particular kind of problem?”

Then the self is not tied to any one representation either.

It becomes the thing maintaining continuity while representations change.

        prose
          │
graph ─── SELF ─── geometry
          │
        program
          │
       narrative

The stable thing is not the box.

The stable thing is the ability to enter a box, operate within its rules, exit it, compare it against another box, and preserve continuity across the transition.

That is very close to what I think Pantheon has been converging toward: not merely a collection of clever concepts, but a representation-switching control architecture layered between an intelligent substrate and the world.